From dcc5371cb8bd806c4ead7f68b934dceb7ed77d3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chiro <chiro2001@163.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 01:15:07 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 256/256] fix(cpu): re-verify the on-disk ELF on every SVE256
 W8A8 memo hit

The identity-keyed decode memo introduced for the 0041 throughput fix
trusted the Artifact object after the first decode: rewriting the ELF
behind the same path was not noticed until the next launch.  (New or
tampered Artifact objects already missed the memo and decoded
fail-closed; only in-place rewrites under one object were invisible.)
Independent verification of 0041 registered this as a boundary.

Memo hits now re-read the ELF through the same hardened reader the full
decode uses (O_NOFOLLOW, single-link regular file) and compare sha256
with the digest captured at decode time.  Any mismatch or read failure
drops the memo entry and falls through to the full decode, so the caller
sees the canonical fail-closed error (recovering automatically if the
original bytes are restored).  The full declaration gate still runs on
every dispatch.

Same-window measurement on the 883,064-byte model-graph ELF: full-decode
miss 2.644 s; checked memo hit 0.642 ms; unchecked memo hit 12 us;
read+sha256 alone 0.43 ms median (0.42 ms min).  The checked hit stays
~4,100x cheaper than a miss and the W8A8 per-op times are unchanged at
the real element counts (5120: 17-23 ms, 30720: 42-70 ms, 65536:
76-164 ms, so the hash is far below the marshalling floor).

Focused tests cover same-object in-place rewrite (same size, larger,
truncated), replacement by an unrelated but valid static AArch64 ELF,
mtime rollback, identical-content inode replacement (accepted), symlink
swap and hardlink swap (both rejected).  The six existing memo-discipline
tests are unchanged.  Known boundary: the check-to-exec TOCTOU window
(including the wire marshalling between check and subprocess exec) still
requires fd-based execution to close, which is a runner/protocol change.
